{"type":"event","event":{"type":"run_end","result":{"finalText":"I'll review both pieces. Since I'm in plan mode, I'll do read-only verification (diffs, source reads, greps) and write findings to the plan file. Note the working tree has renamed `debruijn_vm.cljc` to `debruijn/stack.cljc`, so let me check current state first.\n\n","stopReason":"permission_denied","turnCount":1,"usage":{"inputTokens":16494,"outputTokens":669,"cacheReadTokens":0,"cacheWriteTokens":0},"systemPromptTokens":null,"nextState":{"sessionId":"f951a9f2-8982-4cac-9073-601572a0d74f","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-09-22 08:47:44 GMT\nCoding-Agent: qwen\n# Task: independent review — debruijn-b2 (named-datom lowerer adapter) and the B3 :push fix\nRole: Reviewer\n\nTwo related, uncommitted pieces of work need independent review before commit\n(per this project's standing rule: independent review must complete before\nany commit made while working autonomously).\n\n## Piece 1: B3 fix (already-merged file, working-tree edit)\n\nWork in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld (branch master, HEAD b5bfe94b).\nFile: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc -- run `git diff` to see the change.\nIt adds a `:push` case to `step1`'s opcode dispatch, which was previously\nmissing entirely (a real defect discovered via B2's integration testing:\nany real de-Bruijn-lowered image with a function application throws\n`:not-yet-implemented` the first time it steps past a `:push`).\n\nRead the fix's own comment for the claimed reasoning: the named VM\n(src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc, its `:push` case around line 275) keeps a\nseparate `val` accumulator distinct from its operand stack `St`, so `:push`\nthere commits `val` onto `St`. This machine (debruijn_vm.cljc) has no such\nsplit -- every value-producing case (`:const`, `:load-bound`, `:load-free`,\n`:closure`) already `conj`s its result straight onto `:stack` as it computes\nit. So by the time `step1` reaches a `:push` instruction, the value it would\npush is already on `:stack` -- making `:push` here a no-op that only\nadvances `pc`.\n\nVerify this reasoning is actually correct, not just plausible -- in\nparticular check the `:call` case just below (reads exactly `argc` values\noff the top of `:stack` using `f-pos = total - argc - 1`): would a `:push`\ninstruction ever appear at a point where the value it names is NOT already\nthe top of `:stack` (e.g. after `:branch-false`, `:jump`, or any case not in\nthe small list above)? Grep src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc for `:push` to\nsee every context the shared named lowerer emits it in, and check each one\nagainst B3's opcode set.\n\n## Piece 2: B2 (named-datom lowerer adapter, new files, all uncommitted)\n\nWork in /Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2 (branch debruijn-b2, same\nHEAD, plus the identical :push fix already applied to its own copy of\ndebruijn_vm.cljc). New files:\n\n    src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n    test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n\nRead docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md sections 1-3 and the \"B2: named-datom\nlowerer adapter\" phase box first, for the spec this implements. Then read\nthe new source file in full, then the test file in full.\n\nFocus your review on:\n- The lift correctness law (design section 3, item 6): exact equality with\n  the adapter's own side table, alpha-equivalence with synthesized names,\n  including the duplicate-parameter case `(fn [x x] x)`.\n- Scope reconstruction being a pure function of the image structure, never\n  of `:yin.code/source` (the diagnostic-only AST reference) -- grep for any\n  accidental read of `:yin.code/source` inside the scope-reconstruction\n  logic.\n- Determinism: does running the adapter twice on the same input actually\n  guarantee byte-identical output, or is there any hidden order-dependence\n  (map iteration, set iteration) that could silently vary by host?\n- Every named-lowerer-emitted opcode/shape is handled or explicitly refused\n  -- nothing silently dropped.\n- The negative tests (malformed named vector, out-of-range images) actually\n  exercise the failure path they claim to, not just constructing an object\n  that happens to also pass.\n- Whether corpus-images-load-and-run-on-b3 (the test exercising the :push\n  fix end-to-end) is a real integration test or could pass vacuously.\n\n## Environment\n\nJava 21 and clojure/bb are on PATH via mise; both directories are already\nmise-trusted. Focused runs:\n  clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-vm-test          (in datomworld, for the fix)\n  clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-linearize-test   (in worktree-debruijn-b2, for B2)\nBoth already ran clean for me (14/23 and 10/263 assertions respectively) --\ndo not trust that; verify independently. If kondo/cljstyle are denied, say\nso and stop; do not retry, do not use --dangerously-skip-permissions.\n\n## Verdict\n\nREADY / READY WITH CHANGES / NOT READY, findings as P1 (must fix before\ncommit) / P2 (should fix) / P3 (nit), for each piece separately since they\nmay commit on different timelines.\n\nFinal response beginning exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\nCoding-Agent: qwen"}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1790066916034,"messageId":"6d0a3831-2ec2-4e21-a2a0-2c7837b8046b"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a review task, but we're in plan mode (read-only). Review is actually a read-only activity — we can read files, take diffs, and run tests? Running tests via shell_command is permitted if it's read-only… In plan mode, executing commands that modify state is prohibited. Running tests is essentially read-only, but it does write to the target/ directory. Hmm. In plan mode, you're not supposed to run non-read-only tools. Test execution writes to target/, but is generally considered acceptable for verification purposes? Strictly speaking, plan mode prohibits state modifications. But the re